<LessonLayout title="Workbook &mdash; The Passions and Moral Conscience" cic="CCC 1762-1802" scripture="">

<section class="content">

<h2>Student Workbook</h2>
<p class="big-question">To use after class or in group discussion.</p>

<h3>Remembering the Essentials</h3>

<ol>
<li>What are passions according to the Catechism? Are they good or bad in themselves?</li>
<li>What is moral conscience, and why is it called the &laquo;inner sanctuary&raquo;?</li>
<li>What are the three states of conscience (certain, doubtful, erroneous), and how should we act in each?</li>
<li>How can passions cloud the judgment of conscience?</li>
<li>What does it mean for conscience to be &laquo;formed&raquo;? How is it formed?</li>
</ol>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>For Reflection and Writing</h3>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">1. Which emotion do you most struggle to control? In what situations does it get away from you?</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">2. Have you ever done something you knew was wrong because you &laquo;couldn&rsquo;t help it&raquo;? What happened after?</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">3. How do you form your conscience? What sources do you use to know what is good and what is evil?</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">4. Is there an area of your life where you know your conscience is asking you to change, but you haven&rsquo;t yet?</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">5. How does prayer and confession help you order your passions and form your conscience?</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content" id="activities">

<h3>Practical Activities</h3>

<h4>A. Emotions and Conscience Journal</h4>
<p>For one week, keep a journal where you note:</p>
<ul>
  <li>A situation where you felt a strong emotion (anger, fear, joy, sadness)</li>
  <li>How you responded to that emotion</li>
  <li>Whether your conscience approved or disapproved of your response</li>
  <li>What you would do differently next time</li>
</ul>

<h4>B. Ignatian Examen of Conscience</h4>
<p>Practice St. Ignatius&rsquo;s Examen each night for 5 minutes using these questions:</p>
<ol>
  <li>What am I grateful for today?</li>
  <li>What happened today? (review the day like a movie)</li>
  <li>Where did I feel a strong emotion? How did I respond?</li>
  <li>Where did I fail? Where did I succeed?</li>
  <li>What will I do tomorrow?</li>
</ol>

<h4>C. The Art of Pausing</h4>
<p>This week, before responding to an emotionally intense situation, pause for 5 seconds. Breathe deeply. Ask yourself: &laquo;What does my conscience tell me about this?&raquo; Then respond. Write about the experience.</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Memory Verse</h3>

<div class="bible-passage">
<p>&laquo;Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God&raquo;</p>
<p class="bible-passage__ref">Matthew 5:8</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Prayer for This Week</h3>

<blockquote>
<p>
Lord Jesus,<br />
You who know my heart better than I do,<br />
help me to order my passions.<br />
May anger not govern my words.<br />
May fear not paralyze my faith.<br />
May desire not cloud my reason.<br />
Form my conscience with Your Word,<br />
so that I may distinguish good from evil<br />
and have a pure heart that sees You.<br />
Amen.
</p>
</blockquote>

</section>

